{"yak":{"id":"7de997df-722d-435c-9f9b-fae917dd261a","word":"yak","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"ox-like mammal","tags":["masculine"],"word":"iaque"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Utilization efficiency of dietary protein in the yak differs with diet composition and feeding level, age, sex, body condition score, and animal production level (e.g., growth, lactation). Researchers reported no difference between lactating and dry cows in crude protein digestibility, although lactating yak tend to consume more feed than dry yak.","ref":"2008, Scott R. R. Haskell, Blackwell's Five-Minute Veterinary Consult: Ruminant, John Wiley & Sons, →ISBN, page 619:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[49,52],[306,309],[345,348],[345,349]],"id":"0a5b255d-ddf5-449c-bb86-51d36693ecb3"},{"text":"Attempts are now being made, by selection, to create a new breed of yak (the Datong yak) from such crosses. Hybridization of domestic yak with local cattle, at intermediate elevations, has been practiced for generations. The hybrids inherit some of the good characteristics from each species, but lack the adaptation of the yak to the harsh conditions at higher elevations.","ref":"2004, Wilson G. Pond, Encyclopedia of Animal Science (Print), CRC Press, →ISBN, page 899:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[68,71],[84,87],[134,137],[324,327]],"id":"cd81d0e4-ee53-46a7-82b7-f44a313ddfa3"}],"links":[["ox","ox"],["Himalayas","Himalayas"],["Mongolia","Mongolia"],["Myanmar","Myanmar"],["Tibet","Tibet"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["An ox-like mammal native to the Himalayas, Mongolia, Myanmar, and Tibet with dark, long, and silky hair, a horse-like tail, and a full, bushy mane."],"id":"bb7819bb-6fac-4c89-907e-cee207ef2b48"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"yakut":{"id":"9c0d8fad-dbcf-42a4-ad43-2057f8cc79ea","word":"Yakut","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"language","tags":["m